WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:06.470
There are certain rules
when it comes to movies.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.330
It's always the girl
who survives a killer,

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.926
it has to rain during every funeral,

00:00:12.926 --> 00:00:16.609
and, yes, the third film in a trilogy

00:00:16.609 --> 00:00:18.959
almost always sucks.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.200
And it's not just the terrible ones.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.040
Some of the most critically
acclaimed trilogies

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.860
also suffer from the threequel curse.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:31.073
And it got us wondering,
what's really happening here?

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:34.120
The first thing we needed to find out

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.700
was just how common bad
threequels really are.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.650
And, surprisingly, the
hardest part was deciding

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.543
what exactly constitutes a film trilogy.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:47.370
In our case, it's a set of three films

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.370
that share a continuous
story, character, or theme.

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:54.330
This means that independent
trilogies like "Iron Man"

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.600
and "Annabelle" within an
existing franchise count,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.940
but trilogies that are
continued years later

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.170
like "Bourne" or "Pirates
of the Caribbean" don't.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.470
We then took 70 of the
highest domestically

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:09.550
grossing trilogies in the US

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:13.190
and calculated each film's
average Rotten Tomatoes score

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.410
based on both critic and audience ratings

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:18.300
to get a more accurate depiction.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.440
The list includes the obvious big hitters

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.250
like "The Godfather" and "The Karate Kid,"

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.280
as well as a few unexpected gems

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.680
like Ice Cube's "Barbershop" trilogy.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.005
Eddie: Ricky?
Ricky: What, what?

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:30.455
Eddie: What the hell do you
think you're doing over there?

00:01:30.455 --> 00:01:31.890
Ricky: Hey, look, I'm just
trying to get him clean

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.710
like them Gillette commercials.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:36.050
Narrator: Average out all
the scores for first, second,

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:38.220
and third film, put them on a chart,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.750
and you get something like this.

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:42.950
The first film was the obvious winner,

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.500
with an average score of 71,

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:47.499
followed by the sequel at 58,

00:01:47.499 --> 00:01:50.170
and threequels at 52.

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:53.458
Count them all up, and 64% of trilogies

00:01:53.458 --> 00:01:56.680
had their threequel as their worst film.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:02:00.700
So, why are threequels
almost always the worst ones?

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.420
There are two factors
that come into play here:

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.410
formula and necessity.

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.580
The reason why film
trilogies have been around

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.440
for so long is that they're
a really great structure

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:12.480
to tell a long story,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:16.340
with each film serving as a
beginning, a middle, and an end.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.440
This also means that
each film should serve

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.620
a very specific purpose.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:23.370
No matter the genre, the
first film always serves

00:02:23.370 --> 00:02:25.830
as an origin story that introduces us

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.950
to our heroes and the world they live in.

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:30.200
That's why successful
first movies usually fit

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:32.590
into one of two categories.

00:02:32.590 --> 00:02:36.400
One, adapted materials from
an existing body of work,

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.670
like "The Silence of the Lambs"
or the "Star Trek" reboot,

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:41.540
that accurately capture the essence

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.489
of the original source material,

00:02:43.489 --> 00:02:47.260
or, two, fresh, original IPs,

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.560
like "The Matrix" and "Star Wars,"

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.850
that are quite unlike anything
the audience has seen.

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.870
The sequels always tend
to enjoy more freedom,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.498
since their characters and worlds

00:02:57.498 --> 00:03:00.060
are already established for them.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.350
For this reason, the second film is all

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.620
about heroes facing the greatest challenge

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.750
they've faced yet, whether
it be iconic villains

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:11.100
or a battle that just
seems impossible to win.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.780
This is where the problem
with threequels comes in.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:17.650
There isn't really a formula
for a successful threequel,

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.170
at least not yet.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.550
And while sequels can
usually stand on their own

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.050
and focus on telling a good story...

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:25.200
Bernie: Morning, partner.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.900
Narrator: Threequels have
a lot more to accomplish.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:29.660
They not only have to tell a great story

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:32.070
that tops the films before it,

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:34.580
but they also need to tie every loose end

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.820
and somehow bring a satisfying
conclusion to the saga

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.310
in almost exactly the same
length as the other films.

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:43.080
Studios often try to solve this problem

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.660
with a bigger budget to
visually raise the stakes.

00:03:46.660 --> 00:03:49.300
But it often just comes off as repetitive.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:52.290
Some threequels actually
repeat previous successes

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.480
as a strategy, like how
"Return of the Jedi"

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.780
was really a rehash of "A New Hope"

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.850
but with a bigger Death Star,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:03.120
and "Spider-Man 3," which
crams so many villains

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.084
into one film, just
replicates the same plot beats

00:04:06.084 --> 00:04:07.960
as its previous film.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:11.220
Which brings us to an even
bigger problem: necessity.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.550
Trilogies suffer the most

00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:14.890
when they're just not needed at all.

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:17.940
Skim through 33 of the
worst-rated trilogies,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.880
and you could argue that about 85%

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:22.780
of those films aren't
needed after the first film.

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:25.293
After "The Hangover Part
II" destroyed everything

00:04:25.293 --> 00:04:27.810
the first film did
right by replicating it,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:30.290
there were no loose ends
that needed to be tied up,

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:32.670
nor any separate conclusions to be made.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.670
But two years later, "The
Hangover Part III" still arrived

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:38.680
in the theaters with an
even more abysmal rating.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:40.489
So, despite all of this,

00:04:40.489 --> 00:04:43.980
why does Hollywood keep
making terrible sequels?

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:45.460
The answer seems obvious.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:46.700
Money, right?

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:49.300
Well, the box office shows that each movie

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:51.660
in a trilogy generally makes less money

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.530
than the one that came before,

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.620
but their differences aren't big enough

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.530
to not warrant a threequel.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.789
On average, that third movie still makes

00:04:59.789 --> 00:05:02.270
four times more than its budget.

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.486
And as long as we continue to watch them,

00:05:04.486 --> 00:05:07.480
the problem will likely never be resolved.

00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:09.520
The curse of threequels is an issue

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.170
that perhaps best illustrates the problems

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.320
with how Hollywood approaches a film.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:16.270
Demon: We're back!

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.360
Narrator: A good film can only be achieved

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.000
with a good story.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:22.970
And once studios forget that eternal fact,

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.800
what they're releasing
at the end isn't a film,

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.093
but a product without a soul.

